I’ll first inspect the measurement metadata, pattern format, and the supplied tungstate framework notes, then inventory the installed diffraction/refinement tools. I’ll use those constraints to identify candidate phases before building and validating the final GSAS-II refinement.

The context strongly favors a low-temperature, partially reacted BaCO₃–WO₃ mixture, but I’ll treat that only as a chemical prior. The decisive test will be whether reference structures account for every resolved reflection and whether their refined scales remain stable once instrument/background/specimen effects are introduced.

The line positions already separate cleanly into orthorhombic witherite BaCO₃ and low-symmetry WO₃: for example, the strong BaCO₃ lines near 41.99° and 44.86° map to observed 42.29° and 45.17°, while the WO₃ triplets near 23–25° and 33–35° explain the remaining splitting. I’m now testing alternate WO₃ structure settings and possible BaWO₄ as an added phase by full-profile refinement, rather than deciding from peak lists alone.

The first full-profile test confirms that these two precursor phases explain essentially all major intensity, with an initial quantitative estimate near 55 wt% BaCO₃ / 45 wt% WO₃. I’m comparing the low-symmetry WO₃ references now; several are crystallographically equivalent descriptions, but their oxygen coordinates and symmetry constraints differ enough to affect the profile and structural credibility.

No additional crystalline phase is needed to explain the pattern so far. The dominant remaining mismatch is in relative BaCO₃ reflection intensities—especially the 24° versus 42° lines—which is the signature expected from preferred orientation or surface roughness, not a missing phase. I’m screening the physically allowed texture axes before refining atomic coordinates.

The texture test is specific: BaCO₃ prefers a modest [110] March–Dollase correction (ratio about 0.90), while every WO₃ axis refines back to essentially 1.00. That supports a real BaCO₃ specimen effect rather than indiscriminate profile fitting. I’m now separating common instrumental width from phase-specific crystallite size/microstrain and specimen displacement.

The displacement refinement converged to about −0.70 mm with a separate zero of −0.09°, both inside the stated laboratory ranges. This materially improves the cell metrics and avoids forcing the angular shift into the structures. The current scale estimate is about 53 wt% BaCO₃ / 47 wt% WO₃; I’m treating its formal covariance error as optimistic until coordinate and roughness terms are settled.

The coordinate refinement is computationally heavier because each low-symmetry WO₃ site contributes independent symmetry-allowed shifts. It is still progressing; the refinement is staged with W/Ba first, then oxygen, so I can reject any later overfit if it creates implausible interatomic distances.

Unconstrained oxygen refinement lowered Rwp but displaced several O sites by roughly 0.5–1.5 Å and drove WO₃ Uiso negative, so I rejected it as overfitting. The defensible model keeps the published oxygen framework and refines only Ba/W positions; both monoclinic and triclinic WO₃ remain plausible, with the triclinic model retaining a small profile advantage.

I’m also testing BaWO₄ and the plausible Ba-rich/substoichiometric tungstates as explicit third phases. This is an upper-bound check: each candidate is added to the accepted two-phase project with its own scale, and a credible third phase must improve the profile with a positive, stable fraction rather than merely shifting existing parameters.

The bounded-coordinate refinements strengthen the triclinic assignment: with every atomic displacement limited to the framework’s stated 0.04–0.05 Å reference-mismatch scale, triclinic WO₃ gives Rwp 6.10% versus 6.39% for monoclinic WO₃. I’ll carry the triclinic model, while noting that many oxygen shifts reach their bounds and are therefore not independently reliable.

The phase assignment is now robust: orthorhombic BaCO₃ plus low-symmetry triclinic WO₃. I’m finishing the refinement under physically bounded broadening/strain models, then I’ll export every requested artifact from that single final GSAS-II project and cross-check their internal consistency.

The remaining mismatch is concentrated in the split WO₃ peak clusters rather than in unexplained extra reflections. I’m testing anisotropic microstrain and background terms one block at a time; this avoids the unstable all-at-once least-squares step that drove otherwise physical parameters away from the better solution.

Both phases show only modest but reproducible directional broadening: BaCO₃ along [100] and WO₃ along [001]. I’m retaining those terms because they stay within the synthesis framework’s plausible strain range, while rejecting more elaborate effects that are unsupported or unstable.

The first bounded-coordinate attempt exposed a GSAS-II edge case: when a coordinate hits a hard limit, the stored calculated curve can precede the clamped coordinate update. I’ve isolated that and am rerunning the coordinate block iteratively until no new bound crossings occur; only that reproducible, reloadable model will be eligible for the final project.

The bounded coordinate refinement is behaving as expected now: 44 coordinates reached the allowed structural-mismatch envelope on the first pass. A second pass is running with those held fixed; its result—not the pre-clamp profile—is what determines whether the positional refinement is credible.

